---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import NnMasthead from '../../components/NnMasthead.astro';
import NnPreviousYears from '../../components/NnPreviousYears.astro';
import race from '../../content/race.json';

// The Nightingale Nightmare event theme — the campaign look, and nothing the campaign
// knows that this site does not. See packages/shared/styles/nn-theme.css.
import '@src/shared/styles/nn-theme.css';

// **The race, not one running of it — and this page never names a year.**
//
// A race is the recurring thing; an event is one running of it in one year. This page is the
// first; `/nn/2026/` is the second. That distinction is why publishing 2027 is a row in
// `entries.events` and a new directory of content pages, with **no edit to this file at all**.
//
// **Every link from here to a year page is painted by the Worker**, from
// `entries.current_entry_state('nn')`. There is no `href="/nn/2026/"` anywhere below, and
// there must never be one: it would be the line somebody forgot in 2027, and it would be
// wrong quietly rather than loudly. See `worker/nn-entry.ts`'s `renderNnRaceView`.
//
// ## Which of `race.json`'s keys this page may use, and why the split is not in that file yet
//
// `race.json` describes **the 2026 running**, and it always has: the date, the start time,
// race HQ, the entry fee, the entry window and the ARC permit are all facts about one running
// of this race. **None of them appears on this page any more.** What is left is what is true
// of the race itself — its name, its distance, its field size, and the address to write to.
//
// **The file is not split, and that is deliberate rather than unfinished.** Separating it into
// a race file and a year file is a content change of its own, with its own review, and doing
// it inside a route reorganisation would put two unrelated diffs in one commit. Until then the
// rule is this comment plus the tests: `site.spec.ts` asserts the date and race HQ are on the
// year page and **not** here.
//
// **This page states no date, and that is the one thing somebody may notice is missing.** It
// cannot use `race.json`'s, because that is 2026's; it could be painted from `entry_state()`,
// which returns the event date — but rendering "Sunday 1 November 2026" from a `CivilDate`
// means a second date formatter in a repository whose whole timezone discipline is that there
// is exactly one. The date is one tap away, on the running the block below links to, and that
// is the cheaper side of the trade. **Recorded as a gap rather than a decision to leave
// closed** — see ADR-011.
//
// See docs/architecture/principles.md#stop-and-ask

// One address, and it is a path rather than a subdomain — so at the Squarespace cutover
// the hostname changes and this page does not move.
const canonical = 'https://new.southvillerunningclub.co.uk/nn/';
---

{
  /* **The title names the race and not what the page currently offers.** It read
  "— register your interest", which is one of the two states this page has and became wrong
  the moment the other one was possible. A title is also what a bookmark and a search result
  are called, and both outlive a window. */
}
<Base
  title="Nightingale Nightmare"
  description="Nightingale Nightmare — a tough off-road 10 km in fancy dress, put on by Southville Running Club."
  canonical={canonical}
  theme="nn"
  race={{
    name: race.name,
    privacy: '/nn/privacy/',
    contact: race.contact,
  }}
>
  <NnMasthead slot="masthead" />

  <header class="nn-hero">
    <div class="nn-wrap">
      {
        /* **"presents…" opens the hero now that the wordmark has moved to the masthead.**
        The sentence it belongs to still reads the same way down the page — "Southville
        Running Club" in the header, then "presents…", then the title — which is what
        somebody seeing it gets and, because the header is the first thing in the DOM, what
        somebody hearing it gets too. */
      }
      <p class="nn-presents">presents…</p>

      <h1>{race.name}</h1>

      {
        /* **The three answers somebody wants before they read a sentence.** A real list,
        because that is what it is, with the bullets taken off — a screen reader still says
        "list, three items" and gets the same summary a sighted reader gets from the row.

        The mockup has a fourth item here, a live "238 of 250 places remaining" bar. That is
        demo data, and the database could answer it now — but a number that is wrong within a
        day of anybody entering, cached at the edge and read off a poster-shaped page, is a
        claim the club would have to keep true. How big the race is does not change, so that
        is what this says. Somebody who tries to enter a full race is told plainly, on the
        form, at the moment it matters. */
      }
      <ul class="nn-facts">
        <li>{race.distance}</li>
        <li>{race.places} places</li>
        <li>Fancy dress encouraged</li>
      </ul>

      {
        /* **The lede lost one clause and it was the year-specific one.** It used to say "the
        date is settled", which is a statement about 2026 on a page about the race. That
        sentence is on the year page now, where it is true of the thing being described. */
      }
      {
        /* **The lede lost its second sentence with the button above.** "Leave your name and
        the club will email you once" was an instruction about a form on this page, and the
        form is on the running now. What is left is true of the race whichever year it is run,
        which is all this page is for. */
      }
      <p class="lede">The course has not got any kinder.</p>

      {
        /* **No button in this hero, and that is the form move's tidiest consequence.**

        It used to be "Register your interest", pointing at `#register` — a form that is no
        longer on this page. An anchor to nothing is worse than no anchor: it looks like a
        control, it is reached by keyboard, and pressing it does nothing at all.

        What replaced it is two things that were already there and are both better placed: the
        navigation's button, which is painted with what the window actually allows and sits at
        the top of every page, and the panel below, which is the same action with the date
        attached. A third copy in between was the one furthest from anything it was about. */
      }
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="nn-wrap">
    {
      /* **The race-facts list moved to the year page in one piece.** Every row in it — the
      date, the start time, race HQ, the entry fee, when entries open, the ARC permit — is a
      fact about *one running*, and half of them are `null` for 2026 specifically. A page about
      the race would have been listing next year's unknowns as this year's.

      What is left of it here is the row of three in the hero: the distance, the field size and
      fancy dress, which are what this race is however it is run. */
    }
    {
      /* **The card is the race director's announcement, then the course in full.** It used to
      be her announcement and a five-bullet summary of a separate course page; that page is
      gone and its own copy is below, so the summary went with the thing it was summarising.
      See the note above the course section.

      The mockup has a line here — "Minimum age 18 on race day" — which is inferred from the
      prize categories rather than stated as a rule anywhere. It is not here, and it is not
      softened into "18+ probably" either. */
    }
    {
      /* **The race director's own words, and they open the card rather than replace it.**
       *
       * The brief these came from asked for them *instead of* what follows. What follows is
       * the course and terrain — trail shoes, the climb, no headphones, the water station, and
       * the start not being at race HQ — so taking it out would strip five safety-relevant
       * facts off the only page that now states them. The headphone rule in particular is the
       * club's own additional rule under ARC Rule 81 and is binding on competitors; it is not
       * decoration and it does not come out of a page in a copy edit.
       *
       * **Her paragraph and the section below both name the route, and that is now accepted
       * rather than prevented.** The towpath, Nightingale Valley and Leigh Woods are in her
       * spelling here and in the club's under "Where it goes". This page refused the second
       * copy until the club supplied the course copy in full and asked for it as it stands;
       * the note above that section records the reversal, and `site.spec.ts` asserts the two
       * spellings rather than forbidding one.
       *
       * Her spelling is hers: "10km off road" unhyphenated, "tricked & treated" with an
       * ampersand, "spooktators" lower case here and capitalised as a page name in the bar.
       * Not corrected — see the note in the pull request about the distance now appearing in
       * two spellings on this page, which is hers to settle rather than this file's. */
    }
    <div class="nn-card nn-prose">
      <p class="lede">
        Southville Running Club's 'Spooktacular' Nightingale Nightmare Halloween Run is
        back!
      </p>

      <p>
        A 10km off road run along the towpath, up Nightingale Valley and through Leigh
        Woods. All runners and 'spooktators' will be tricked & treated, and Halloween
        fancy dress is strongly encouraged!
      </p>

      {
        /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         * "Know what you are in for" and `NnRaceSummary` were here, and they have gone
         * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         * **Not dropped — superseded.** The five bullets were the short form of the course
         * page, extracted from it so the two could not drift while both existed. The course
         * page's own prose is below now, supplied by the club as the wording this page should
         * carry, and it states every one of those five things at greater length. Keeping both
         * would have printed the headphone rule and the climb twice inside one card.
         *
         * **The component is untouched and still renders on `/nn/2026/`**, which is the page
         * with the entry form on it and the page that wants the short form: somebody about to
         * pay wants five lines, not four sections. So it is no longer *shared* — and the note
         * at the head of `NnRaceSummary.astro` says so, because a component whose whole
         * argument was "both pages need exactly these five" needs to stop claiming that the
         * moment one of them stops.
         *
         * **One bullet was in the summary and is not in the supplied copy** — that the start
         * and the finish are not at race HQ. It is carried into "What is on the route" below
         * rather than lost, because it is the fact that stops somebody driving to the wrong
         * place on the morning. It is the club's existing wording, moved rather than written.
         * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
      }

      {
        /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         * The course and terrain — the course page's own copy, in full
         * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         * **This was `/nn/course/`, and it is the whole of it rather than a summary.** The
         * wording below was supplied by the club as what this page should say, and it is
         * transcribed rather than edited — its own spellings, its own emphasis, its own
         * three-section shape. `/nn/course/` 301s here now, so this is the only place any of
         * it is stated.
         *
         * **The route sentence is here on purpose and it is the one thing to look at twice.**
         * The race director's paragraph above already names the towpath, Nightingale Valley
         * and Leigh Woods in her own spelling, so this page now states the route in two
         * wordings. `site.spec.ts` used to refuse exactly that — "one route, one spelling per
         * page" — and the guard is inverted rather than deleted, so the second copy is a
         * recorded decision that somebody can find, rather than a thing that crept in.
         *
         * **The headings are `<h3>` under this `<h2>`.** `course.astro` carried an
         * `<h1>Course and terrain</h1>` with `<h2>`s beneath it; this page's `<h1>` is the
         * race, so everything drops one level. A second `<h1>` would be an accessibility
         * defect on a site that runs axe at zero, and `<h2>`s here would make the three
         * sections siblings of the section they belong to.
         *
         * **The distance is `race.json`'s and not a typed "10 km".** It is the same fact the
         * hero's fact list reads, and a second literal is a second thing to change.
         * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
      }
      <h2>The course and terrain</h2>

      {
        /* **`race.distance` is already "10 km, off-road"**, so the supplied line's opening is
        the value on its own. Typing the ", off-road" as well rendered "10 km, off-road,
        off-road — and not a fast one." — which is what happens whenever a literal is written
        beside a value that already contains it. The distance has one home and this reads it. */
      }
      <p>
        {race.distance} — and not a fast one. This is a race you finish rather than a race you
        set a time on.
      </p>

      <h3>Where it goes</h3>

      <p>
        The route runs along the towpath, turns up Nightingale Valley, and carries on
        through Leigh Woods.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Nightingale Valley is the climb.</strong> It is the part people remember, and
        the part that decides how the rest of your race feels. There is no clever way to run
        it — go up steadily, and enjoy how much better everything looks from the top.
      </p>

      <h3>What it is like underfoot</h3>

      <p>
        Tough, and properly off-road. The ground is uneven, rocky in places and rooty in
        others, and it is slippery when wet. It is Bristol, in November. Plan for wet.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Trail shoes are recommended.</strong> Road shoes are acceptable, and people
        run it in them every year. They are working harder than they need to.
      </p>

      <h3>What is on the route</h3>

      <ul class="nn-arrows">
        <li>
          One water station on the route at approximately halfway, and one at the finish.
        </li>
        <li>
          Marshals throughout — and a prize afterwards for the station the runners vote
          the best.
        </li>
        {
          /* **The rule is the club's own words and it is left at that.** "This one is
          absolute" was on the course page and has gone — not because the rule has softened,
          but because the fee structure carries a place for a visually impaired runner's guide
          and whether a guide is an exception to this has never been decided. "Absolute"
          settles that question in passing, in markup, which is not where it should be
          settled. Stating the rule and stopping leaves the committee the decision and leaves
          every runner reading it in no doubt about what is being asked of them. */
        }
        <li><strong>No headphones of any type during the race.</strong></li>
        {
          /* **The one line here that did not come from the course page.** It was
          `NnRaceSummary`'s fifth bullet, which this section replaced, and it is the only one
          of the five the supplied copy does not restate. It is the fact that stops somebody
          driving to the wrong place on the morning, so it is carried rather than dropped —
          the club's existing wording, moved. **The time is deliberately not in it**: the walk
          down is a row in this year's schedule, on `/nn/<year>/race-day/`, and it has already
          moved once. */
        }
        <li>
          <strong>The start and the finish are not at race HQ.</strong> Everybody walks down
          together beforehand, so there is nothing to find on your own.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    {
      /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       * The year panel — when is it, and can I enter
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       * **A panel, not a link.** This is the front door's answer to the two questions
       * somebody arriving from a shared link actually has, in the order they have them: the
       * date first and largest, then whether they can enter yet.
       *
       * **Ships hidden and empty; the Worker fills it in** from
       * `entries.current_entry_state('nn')`, so 2027 is a row in `entries.events` rather than
       * an edit to this file. There is no `href="/nn/2026/"` anywhere below and there must
       * never be one — see the note at the head of this file.
       *
       * **A database this Worker cannot reach paints nothing**, so the panel stays hidden and
       * the page is the summary card and the interest form. One fewer door in the front door,
       * which is the right side of that trade against a link to a year nobody confirmed.
       *
       * ## Two states, one shape
       *
       * The layout does not move when entries open, so nobody has to relearn the page at the
       * one moment they are trying to do something. Only two things change: the action goes
       * from a quiet outline to the filled button every other primary control on this site
       * uses, and the fee line appears under it. **The difference in prominence is the
       * message** — there is deliberately no badge and no banner saying "open", because the
       * button already says it and a page that said it twice would be shouting.
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    }

    {
      /* **`nn-panel` rather than `nn-card`, and the swap is the whole restyle.** The club asked
      for this panel in the campaign's aubergine rather than on white. Changing the surface
      changes every colour inside it — see the contrast table over `.nn-panel-year` in
      `nn-theme.css`, where each one is recomputed rather than re-picked.

      **One of them is fixed by this line alone and is worth knowing about.** The action is
      `.nn-ghost`, whose base colour is bone and which `nn-theme.css` overrides to blood *scoped
      to `.nn-card`* — precisely so a ghost on a dark surface keeps the light one. Dropping
      `nn-card` here lets that base rule apply again, so the button goes from blood at 1.72:1 on
      aubergine, which is all but invisible, to bone at 14.51:1, without a new rule. */
    }
    <section
      class="nn-panel nn-panel-year"
      data-nn-panel
      hidden
      aria-labelledby="next-race"
    >
      {
        /* **An `<h2>` in the display face, and the one thing on this card that may be.** It
        was a 13px uppercase `<p>` — a quiet eyebrow, which is right on a white card and reads
        as unfinished on the campaign's aubergine. Nosifer is the poster face, and the rule at
        the head of `nn-theme.css` is that it is only ever used for **a heading that carries no
        instruction**. "The next race" is exactly that: it names the block and asks nothing.

        **The date immediately below it stays in the text face**, and the note over
        `.nn-panel-date` in that stylesheet calls this out by name as "the one place it would be
        most tempting to break" the rule. A date is a fact somebody acts on. It is also still
        the largest thing here — see the size note below.

        **It is an `<h2>` rather than a styled `<p>` because the card had no heading at all.**
        The other `.nn-panel`s use one; this one leaned on `aria-labelledby` pointing at a
        paragraph, which names the region correctly and leaves it out of the document outline.
        The page reads h1, h2 (the course and terrain) with three h3s under it, then this — no
        level skipped, which is what the axe sweep at zero violations needs. */
      }
      <h2 class="nn-panel-label" id="next-race">The next race</h2>

      {
        /* **The biggest thing in the panel.** Somebody arriving from a shared link
        asks "when is it" first, and everything else here is smaller than this on purpose —
        including the heading above it, which is why that one is capped below this one's size
        rather than taking `.nn-panel h2`'s own.

        Painted rather than read from `race.json`: that file's date belongs to the 2026
        running, and this page is about the race. The value comes through `packages/shared`'s
        one date formatter — see `formatEventDate`. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-date" data-nn-panel-date></p>

      <p class="nn-panel-facts">
        <span data-nn-panel-time></span> · {race.distance} · {race.places} places
      </p>

      <hr class="nn-panel-rule" />

      {
        /* **One control in two weights**, not two controls with one hidden. A second button
        revealed beside a hidden one is two things to keep in step and two places for a label
        to go stale; the Worker swaps this one's class and its destination. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-actions">
        <a class="nn-ghost" href="" data-nn-panel-action data-nn-panel-link="year">
          The race this year
        </a>
      </p>

      {
        /* **Before entries open, and it names no month.** "Entries open in September" would be
        a claim about a date nobody has confirmed — the entry open and close times are on this
        repository's unconfirmed list and may not appear anywhere. What is true without
        confirmation is that they are not open and that the club will say when they are.

        When the committee settles the opening time, the honest home for it is
        `entries.events.entries_open_at` — which `entry_state()` deliberately does not return
        yet, for exactly this reason — and this line becomes painted like the date above it. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-note" data-nn-panel-shut>
        Entries are not open yet. Leave your name and the club will email you the day they
        are.
      </p>

      {
        /* The fee line, and it is the database's numbers or nothing. `entries.fees` is the
        only place a price exists, and this is painted from the same read the rest of the
        panel is. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-note" data-nn-panel-open hidden>
        <span data-nn-panel-fees></span>
      </p>

      {
        /* **"Race instructions" and "Spooktators" were here and have gone, on request.**
         *
         * They were painted from the same read as everything else on this card, and nothing
         * about the Worker changed when they came out: `renderNnRaceView` still registers
         * `[data-nn-panel-link="race-day"]` and `[data-nn-panel-link="spectators"]`, and
         * `HTMLRewriter` simply finds nothing to paint. That is why no handler was deleted —
         * `/nn/2026/` uses the same two selectors, and removing them would have taken that
         * page's links out with these.
         *
         * **Nobody is stranded, which is the only reason this is a copy decision rather than a
         * navigation one.** Both destinations are in the bar at the top of every campaign page,
         * as "Race info" and "Spooktators", painted for the current running exactly as these
         * were. The card is left saying one thing — when the race is, and the way in to it —
         * instead of four, and the button is now the only control on it.
         *
         * `.nn-panel-links` stays in `nn-theme.css`: `/nn/2026/` has no such list today, but
         * the rule is the panel's rather than this page's, and its colour is part of the
         * measured set the contrast test resolves. */
      }
    </section>

    <NnPreviousYears />

    {
      /* **The "What this page proves" block was here, and it is gone.**
       *
       * A `<dl>` of the database timestamp, a pipeline-check marker, "Served by: Cloudflare
       * Workers, static assets plus one handler" and "Application: apps/main" — directly
       * below the form somebody hands over £17 and their emergency contact on.
       *
       * Every one of those round trips is still made, still by this Worker, still in the real
       * runtime, and the smoke test still fails the deploy if either breaks. They are answered
       * at `/_health` now, as JSON. **The check was never the problem; its audience was** — this
       * page's reader is a runner deciding whether the club can be trusted with a card number,
       * and a page that reports its own plumbing reads as a page that is not finished.
       *
       * **This branch had moved it to `/nn/2026/` rather than removed it**, which was the
       * wrong half of the same instinct: a page a runner reads is not where a platform
       * reports on itself, and the year page is read by more of them than this one. Both
       * copies are gone; the round trips are unchanged.
       *
       * See `packages/shared/src/health-report.ts` and `worker/index.ts`. */
    }

    {
      /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       * This page's own `<footer>` was here, and it has gone into the club's
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       * **The same move `/nn/2026/` made, and for the same reason.** It held three things —
       * the club's name, the contact address, and the race's privacy notice — and all three
       * are in the one footer below now, grouped under the race's name through `Base`'s
       * `race` prop. Two stacked `<footer>` elements were the thing that move removed there,
       * and this page had the same pair.
       *
       * **The attribution went rather than moving.** "Southville Running Club." is the
       * masthead's wordmark at the top of the page and the club footer's own subject at the
       * bottom; a page does not need to name its own club a third time, between the two.
       *
       * **The wayfinding clause went earlier and still has nothing replacing it.** It read
       * "This page is about the race itself; each year's running has its own, linked above",
       * and the link it promised is the year panel — which ships `hidden` and is revealed only
       * when the Worker can reach the database, so on the failure path the sentence pointed at
       * nothing. That the panel is the *only* route from here to the year page, and that a
       * Worker which cannot reach Postgres leaves this page with no route to it at all, is
       * issue #71 rather than something to patch with a sentence.
       *
       * **What this page passes and `/nn/2026/` does not is the absence of `terms`.** The
       * entry terms live at `/nn/<year>/terms/` and this page may not name a year, so the
       * group renders two links here and three there. See the prop's own note in
       * `SiteFooter.astro`.
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    }
  </div>

  {
    /* The club's own artwork, at the foot of the page. **Empty on purpose** — it is a CSS
    background, not an `<img>`, because it is decoration that states nothing and because an
    image with intrinsic dimensions is briefly 1080 pixels wide before the stylesheet lands.
    See the artwork section of nn-theme.css. */
  }
  <div class="nn-hands" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</Base>
